{0 Transaction DSL}


The Transaction DSL composes multi‑step workflows that run atomically. It wraps [Session.begin_transaction/commit/rollback] and provides [let*], [and+], [catch], and helpers. This page mirrors [ocaml/src/transaction_dsl.{mli,ml}] and example usage in [ocaml/examples/simple_queries.ml].

Core concepts
- Type: ['a Transaction_dsl.t]
- Build with [return], [fail], [let*], [let+], [and+]
- Execute with [Transaction_dsl.run tx session]

Running a transaction
{[
open Neo4j_eio
open Transaction_dsl

let tx =
  let* rows = exec_query_builder (Query_builder.raw "RETURN 1 AS ok") in
  match rows with
  | [r] -> (match Record.at_int r "ok" with Ok 1L -> return () | _ -> fail (Error.ClientError {code="Client.Decode"; message="expected 1"}))
  | _ -> fail (Error.ClientError {code="Client.Shape"; message="expected one row"})

let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
    let cfg = Config.of_env () in
    Eio.Switch.run @@ fun sw ->
      match Session.with_session ~sw ~net:env#net cfg (fun s -> run tx s) with
      | Ok () -> ()
      | Error e -> Printf.eprintf "tx failed: %s\n" (Error.to_string e)
]}

Composing queries
- Use [and+] to run queries whose results you want to combine.

{[
let tx =
  let+ r1 = exec_query_builder (Query_builder.raw "RETURN 1 AS a")
  and+ r2 = exec_query_builder (Query_builder.raw "RETURN 2 AS b") in
  match r1, r2 with
  | [ra], [rb] ->
      (match Record.at_int ra "a", Record.at_int rb "b" with
       | Ok a, Ok b -> Printf.printf "sum=%Ld\n" (Int64.add a b)
       | _ -> ())
  | _ -> ()
]}

Control flow and errors
- [when_], [unless], [iter], [map] help structure conditional and batch logic.
- Use [catch] or [try_with] for fallback paths; both perform a rollback and start a new transaction for the handler branch.

{[
let tx =
  catch
    (let* () = exec_query_builder_unit (Query_builder.raw "INVALID") in return ())
    (fun err ->
      Printf.printf "caught: %s\n" (Error.to_string err);
      exec_query_builder_unit (Query_builder.raw "RETURN 1"))
]}

Commit/rollback signals
- Normally [run] auto‑commits on success and rolls back on error.
- Use [commit]/[rollback] to signal intent explicitly when you want to return a value alongside control.

Interoperability
- You can mix direct [Session] calls by using [get_session] to obtain the session and [lift_result] to integrate result values.
